approaches.fit_notes:
This is probably the best cost/benefit option for cell-dev, because a GA has no value function but has cheaper and more honest learnability signals:
- the user's GRADIENT TEST: the fraction of random mutants of current elites that improve at z>2 without shrinking or clicking less. This is a direct, capability-agnostic learning-potential score per world;
- the slope of the best F over the last k generations;
- the information-controller regret from the PAIRED entry.
PLR-style replay probability can be proportional to a rank mix of learnability and regret. ACCEL edits are small mutations of world keys, prey parameters or blob layouts. Keep the plan's 25% base lives (Robust PLR's 'curated only' rule would otherwise drop the small-body rung). Cost is low: scores come from lives already run for selection, plus periodic mutant panels.
